00:06:35.160 --> 00:06:36.360
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Oh, yeah.

00:06:36.360 --> 00:06:36.640
You're allowed.

00:06:36.640 --> 00:06:37.010
MARK MANDEL: There's no limit.

00:06:37.010 --> 00:06:38.880
FRANCESC CAMPOY:
Yeah, you're allowed.

00:06:38.880 --> 00:06:39.880
VARUN TALWAR: Well, yes.

00:06:39.880 --> 00:06:42.340
If I had to say it
in like one line,

00:06:42.340 --> 00:06:48.330
it would be, gRPC is a high
performance and efficient,

00:06:48.330 --> 00:06:50.570
now open source
RPC framework, that

00:06:50.570 --> 00:06:54.230
puts mobile and HTTP/2 first.

00:06:54.230 --> 00:06:58.680
So it's really a general
purpose RPC framework

00:06:58.680 --> 00:07:02.390
that anybody can use
to connect to systems,

00:07:02.390 --> 00:07:05.591
and it supports a
whole ton of languages.

00:07:05.591 --> 00:07:06.340
MARK MANDEL: Cool.

00:07:06.340 --> 00:07:07.881
So let's maybe go
a little higher up.

00:07:07.881 --> 00:07:08.960
Like we say RPC.

00:07:08.960 --> 00:07:10.480
What does RPC stand for?

00:07:10.480 --> 00:07:13.367
What is that, and why
do people use that?

00:07:13.367 --> 00:07:14.200
VARUN TALWAR: Right.

00:07:14.200 --> 00:07:18.570
So as many of you in this
audience I suppose would know,

00:07:18.570 --> 00:07:21.320
RPC stands for Remote
Procedure Call.

00:07:21.320 --> 00:07:24.940
I'll take you back to CS101 in
our undergrad schools, which

00:07:24.940 --> 00:07:28.710
is, basically the way we
were taught, all of us

00:07:28.710 --> 00:07:32.000
were taught RPCs was, you
know, you have a process,

00:07:32.000 --> 00:07:33.790
but it's not running
on the same machine.

00:07:33.790 --> 00:07:35.600
It's a remote process.

00:07:35.600 --> 00:07:37.050
So how do you call it?

00:07:37.050 --> 00:07:39.520
You basically--
RPC is a technology

00:07:39.520 --> 00:07:44.320
where you define
what that service is.

00:07:44.320 --> 00:07:47.200
You take all the arguments
from the calling machine,

00:07:47.200 --> 00:07:49.150
you serialize,
you do marshaling,

00:07:49.150 --> 00:07:50.260
send them over the wire.

00:07:50.260 --> 00:07:53.220
There's an unmarshaling
happens, takes the arguments,

00:07:53.220 --> 00:07:55.610
does the processing,
and sends back.

00:07:55.610 --> 00:07:56.110
Right?

00:07:56.110 --> 00:08:00.520
So that's how, conventionally,
we've all been taught RPC.

00:08:00.520 --> 00:08:04.112
And that's what, at
the core of it, it is.

00:08:04.112 --> 00:08:05.720
You know, request response.

00:08:05.720 --> 00:08:07.380
But these days,
RPC frameworks have

00:08:07.380 --> 00:08:08.720
become pretty sophisticated.

00:08:08.720 --> 00:08:11.220
They can do synchronous,
asynchronous streaming

00:08:11.220 --> 00:08:12.444
and all of those good things.

00:08:12.444 --> 00:08:13.360
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Nice.

00:08:13.360 --> 00:08:17.180
So by the description you gave,
if I gave that description

00:08:17.180 --> 00:08:22.150
to anyone, I'm sure that 99%
of people could say, oh, REST.

00:08:22.150 --> 00:08:23.467
What is the difference with it?

00:08:23.467 --> 00:08:24.300
VARUN TALWAR: Right.

00:08:24.300 --> 00:08:29.940
So REST has pretty much become
a standard in how people

00:08:29.940 --> 00:08:32.970
expose their sort of services.

00:08:32.970 --> 00:08:38.330
You know, used to be the SOAPs
and WSDLs and sort of REST

00:08:38.330 --> 00:08:41.559
really to con with the growth
of internet and sort of browsers

00:08:41.559 --> 00:08:45.120
and mobile phones interacting
with internet services.

00:08:45.120 --> 00:08:48.065
Primarily because,
you know, all it was

00:08:48.065 --> 00:08:49.950
was sort of HTTP methods.

00:08:49.950 --> 00:08:50.710
Right?

00:08:50.710 --> 00:08:54.360
And they could flow through
proxies and firewalls,

00:08:54.360 --> 00:08:58.240
and easy to call, easy to test.

00:08:58.240 --> 00:09:00.640
So REST is great.

00:09:00.640 --> 00:09:04.910
This is-- you know,
by no means gRPC is

00:09:04.910 --> 00:09:06.160
saying that REST is not good.

00:09:06.160 --> 00:09:07.850
REST continues to
dominate, and it's

00:09:07.850 --> 00:09:10.420
great for a lot of use cases.

00:09:10.420 --> 00:09:14.605
As a lot of you know, REST
is very resource oriented.

00:09:14.605 --> 00:09:17.520
It stands for Representational
State Transfer.

00:09:17.520 --> 00:09:20.830
You basically define the
resources on the server side,

00:09:20.830 --> 00:09:25.136
and HTTP GET, PUT,
POST methods on top

00:09:25.136 --> 00:09:26.260
of each of those resources.

00:09:26.260 --> 00:09:30.080
Basically, cloud operations
on all your server

00:09:30.080 --> 00:09:34.030
side resources is how REST
works, which is great.

00:09:34.030 --> 00:09:38.690
What we have seen is
when people really,

00:09:38.690 --> 00:09:41.190
really care about
performance, and they

00:09:41.190 --> 00:09:44.420
care about what is being--
what format is being carried

00:09:44.420 --> 00:09:50.030
on the wire, and trying
to squeeze out the best

00:09:50.030 --> 00:09:52.820
performance, that is
where things like gRPC

00:09:52.820 --> 00:09:57.670
shine, where we serialize
into binary over the wire,

00:09:57.670 --> 00:10:01.520
as opposed to carrying JSON,
when we've seen, in some cases,

00:10:01.520 --> 00:10:07.600
even crazy improvements like
100x, just by changing that.

00:10:07.600 --> 00:10:10.680
The other thing is, for a data
center and cross data center--

00:10:10.680 --> 00:10:13.710
large scale operations,
like the internet companies

00:10:13.710 --> 00:10:18.460
of the world-- one,
performance is critical.

00:10:18.460 --> 00:10:20.520
Second, there are
a lot of use cases

00:10:20.520 --> 00:10:24.050
where it's not just request
response these days.

00:10:24.050 --> 00:10:25.980
It's like with
push notifications,

00:10:25.980 --> 00:10:29.510
and streaming data
sources, and streaming data

00:10:29.510 --> 00:10:32.280
from all kinds of
connected devices,

00:10:32.280 --> 00:10:34.300
people really want
bidirectional streaming--

00:10:34.300 --> 00:10:36.740
both client to server,
and server to client.

00:10:36.740 --> 00:10:42.190
And this is where,
again, gRPC is great.

00:10:42.190 --> 00:10:44.980
Because it allows
you to establish one

00:10:44.980 --> 00:10:49.590
TCP connection and send a ton
of messages on both sites,

00:10:49.590 --> 00:10:52.170
with having efficiency
on the wire.

00:10:52.170 --> 00:10:54.030
So I think these are
some of the use cases

00:10:54.030 --> 00:10:59.700
where we are seeing
adoption of gRPC.

00:10:59.700 --> 00:11:01.760
The other big
difference, I would say,

00:11:01.760 --> 00:11:05.280
is-- in terms of
the mental model is,

00:11:05.280 --> 00:11:09.290
if I were to put it in like
very, very simple terms-- REST

00:11:09.290 --> 00:11:14.070
is very much nouns oriented, and
gRPC is kind of verbs oriented.

00:11:14.070 --> 00:11:14.570
Right?

00:11:14.570 --> 00:11:17.440
So the way you
define your services

00:11:17.440 --> 00:11:20.560
is what your systems
and services can do.

00:11:20.560 --> 00:11:25.150
And this is the service
definition language,

00:11:25.150 --> 00:11:27.190
which is Google's
implementation is

00:11:27.190 --> 00:11:30.910
based on proto, because protobuf
is what Google uses internally.

00:11:30.910 --> 00:11:33.450
But gRPC as a
protocol is designed

00:11:33.450 --> 00:11:37.470
so that you can use any IDL--
any Interface Definition

00:11:37.470 --> 00:11:40.840
Language, in short, IDL--
and basically define

00:11:40.840 --> 00:11:43.920
your services using any IDL.

00:11:43.920 --> 00:11:47.340
And we are just the transport
which will carry our messages

00:11:47.340 --> 00:11:48.410
efficiently.

00:11:48.410 --> 00:11:49.502
Right?

00:11:49.502 --> 00:11:51.600
MARK MANDEL: So
you mentioned IDL.

00:11:51.600 --> 00:11:53.520
What does IDL stand for?

00:11:53.520 --> 00:11:57.143
VARUN TALWAR: Yeah, so IDL is
Interface Definition Language.

00:11:57.143 --> 00:12:00.090
The implementation
at Google for gRPC

00:12:00.090 --> 00:12:05.460
uses protobuf, which is a
way you define your services.

00:12:05.460 --> 00:12:10.430
So to take it a step further,
you define your methods

00:12:10.430 --> 00:12:12.000
in proto.

00:12:12.000 --> 00:12:14.846
We give you a compiler,
using which you--

00:12:14.846 --> 00:12:16.780
which is called protoc.

00:12:16.780 --> 00:12:18.190
You compile using that.

00:12:18.190 --> 00:12:20.149
You get your client
and server side stubs,

00:12:20.149 --> 00:12:21.190
and then you communicate.

00:12:21.190 --> 00:12:24.050
At a high level,
that's what it is.

00:12:24.050 --> 00:12:26.633
FRANCESC CAMPOY: That sounds a
little bit like Swagger, right?

00:12:28.989 --> 00:12:29.780
VARUN TALWAR: Yes--

00:12:29.780 --> 00:12:31.863
MARK MANDEL: Like the five
that you're describing,

00:12:31.863 --> 00:12:32.799
methods and resources.

00:12:32.799 --> 00:12:33.590
VARUN TALWAR: Yeah.

00:12:33.590 --> 00:12:37.620
So yeah, Swagger is great.

00:12:37.620 --> 00:12:39.710
So yes and no would
be the short answer.

00:12:39.710 --> 00:12:40.820
So Swagger is--

00:12:40.820 --> 00:12:43.246
MARK MANDEL: The easy answer.

00:12:43.246 --> 00:12:44.870
VARUN TALWAR: Swagger
is great in terms

00:12:44.870 --> 00:12:52.720
of its tooling around doc
gen, testing, sharing,

00:12:52.720 --> 00:12:55.490
server specification and so on.

00:12:55.490 --> 00:12:59.290
And it's greatly adopted
for rush services.

00:12:59.290 --> 00:13:05.210
But we think of Swagger as a
great, complementary technology

00:13:05.210 --> 00:13:08.230
that we want to actually
lean in and adopt

00:13:08.230 --> 00:13:15.020
by having ways to convert
some of these serve IDL

00:13:15.020 --> 00:13:17.920
files, protofiles, into Swagger.

00:13:17.920 --> 00:13:22.940
And then, allowing people to use
all kinds of downstream Swagger

00:13:22.940 --> 00:13:25.540
tool chains-- you know, tools
that they're familiar with,

00:13:25.540 --> 00:13:27.500
so they don't have
to really change

00:13:27.500 --> 00:13:31.340
the way they're using things,
in terms of doc generation,

00:13:31.340 --> 00:13:34.760
testing, things like
API Explorer, and so on.

00:13:34.760 --> 00:13:38.030
FRANCESC CAMPOY: So did that
mean that, at some point,

00:13:38.030 --> 00:13:40.690
we might have some
protocol buffer definitions

00:13:40.690 --> 00:13:43.830
and some tool to say, generate
the Swagger specification,

00:13:43.830 --> 00:13:47.090
make a response to that,
and then use my swagger

00:13:47.090 --> 00:13:50.460
explorer to do exactly-- like
to go over my protocol buffer

00:13:50.460 --> 00:13:51.659
definitions.

00:13:51.659 --> 00:13:52.950
VARUN TALWAR: You read my mind.

00:13:52.950 --> 00:13:55.010
FRANCESC CAMPOY: That's awesome.

00:13:55.010 --> 00:13:56.599
Is that actually coming?

00:13:56.599 --> 00:13:57.640
Can you say a little bit?

00:13:57.640 --> 00:13:59.570
Like is it something
that you are working on?

00:13:59.570 --> 00:14:00.320
VARUN TALWAR: Yes.

00:14:00.320 --> 00:14:03.670
It's actually being worked on.

00:14:03.670 --> 00:14:07.864
I can go a step further and
say it's being used internally.

00:14:07.864 --> 00:14:08.780
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Nice.

00:14:08.780 --> 00:14:11.200
VARUN TALWAR: So it's
not like just an idea.

00:14:11.200 --> 00:14:12.620
It's actually--

00:14:12.620 --> 00:14:13.875
MARK MANDEL: It kind of works.

00:14:13.875 --> 00:14:14.770
VARUN TALWAR: It kind of works.

00:14:14.770 --> 00:14:15.269
Yeah.

00:14:15.269 --> 00:14:17.660
So coming soon is
what I can easily say.

00:14:17.660 --> 00:14:18.240
FRANCESC CAMPOY:
That's very good.

00:14:18.240 --> 00:14:19.260
I'm very excited about that.

00:14:19.260 --> 00:14:20.426
MARK MANDEL: That's awesome.

00:14:20.426 --> 00:14:23.970
OK, so just to kind
of put a bow around it

00:14:23.970 --> 00:14:26.350
a little bit, so
development flow with gRPC.

00:14:26.350 --> 00:14:28.830
So we've got this IDL,
[INAUDIBLE] language.

00:14:28.830 --> 00:14:30.806
So I write my protobuf file.

00:14:30.806 --> 00:14:32.180
What does that
kind of look like?

00:14:32.180 --> 00:14:34.596
What does that-- I know it's
kind of hard to say by voice.

00:14:34.596 --> 00:14:39.080
But what-- sort of general
concepts, how does that look?

00:14:39.080 --> 00:14:41.690
VARUN TALWAR: Yeah, so
proto-- and you know,

00:14:41.690 --> 00:14:44.470
if you go to grpc.io, we
have great "Hello, World"

00:14:44.470 --> 00:14:48.590
examples to actually
get you started on that.

00:14:48.590 --> 00:14:52.686
But essentially, you say,
service any method name-- let's

00:14:52.686 --> 00:14:54.070
say it's "Hello, World!"

00:14:54.070 --> 00:14:55.910
You say a service.
"Hello, World!"

00:14:55.910 --> 00:14:57.910
You say it's an RPC.

00:14:57.910 --> 00:15:00.870
Input, you know-- let's say
I want to say, "Hello, Mark."

00:15:00.870 --> 00:15:05.510
So I'll string name, and you
know, output string as well.

00:15:05.510 --> 00:15:07.090
So that's all you're defining.

00:15:07.090 --> 00:15:10.750
Then you say your message, which
is the actual payload that will

00:15:10.750 --> 00:15:12.650
be used for the communication.

00:15:12.650 --> 00:15:14.600
So you basically define
service and message.

00:15:14.600 --> 00:15:19.980
You can think of it in sort
of a terms as your methods

00:15:19.980 --> 00:15:22.850
and your data schema.

00:15:22.850 --> 00:15:26.790
And you define those,
and we give you

00:15:26.790 --> 00:15:31.090
a compiler to basically
compile and get

00:15:31.090 --> 00:15:34.080
code generated in 10 languages.

00:15:34.080 --> 00:15:34.830
MARK MANDEL: Nice.

00:15:34.830 --> 00:15:37.510
VARUN TALWAR: So, and you know,
I didn't say they're wrong.

00:15:37.510 --> 00:15:38.750
It's actually 10 languages.

00:15:38.750 --> 00:15:39.313
FRANCESC CAMPOY:
We're not going to ask

00:15:39.313 --> 00:15:40.265
you to say it, all of those.

00:15:40.265 --> 00:15:41.520
VARUN TALWAR: All of them.

00:15:41.520 --> 00:15:42.940
Pretty much any language--

00:15:42.940 --> 00:15:44.284
MARK MANDEL: I'm actually
wondering if I can remember off

00:15:44.284 --> 00:15:45.150
the top of my head.

00:15:45.150 --> 00:15:46.566
I want to see if
I get this right.

00:15:46.566 --> 00:15:52.892
I know there's C,
C++, PHP, Java, Ruby--

00:15:52.892 --> 00:15:54.350
FRANCESC CAMPOY:
Are you going to--

00:15:54.350 --> 00:15:54.840
MARK MANDEL: Go.

00:15:54.840 --> 00:15:55.800
FRANCESC CAMPOY: OK, thank you.

00:15:55.800 --> 00:15:56.790
I was like, really?

00:15:56.790 --> 00:15:58.620
VARUN TALWAR:
Francesc [INAUDIBLE].

00:15:58.620 --> 00:16:00.809
FRANCESC CAMPOY:
Python, Objective C.

00:16:00.809 --> 00:16:01.850
MARK MANDEL: Objective C.

00:16:01.850 --> 00:16:02.160
VARUN TALWAR: Node.

00:16:02.160 --> 00:16:02.530
MARK MANDEL: Node.

00:16:02.530 --> 00:16:04.113
I know that's the
one we were missing.

00:16:04.113 --> 00:16:04.704
There we go.

00:16:04.704 --> 00:16:05.620
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Yeah.

00:16:05.620 --> 00:16:08.410
OK, so basically at the
end what we're doing

00:16:08.410 --> 00:16:11.090
is-- what you write in the
protocol buffer definition is

00:16:11.090 --> 00:16:15.570
basically like an interface,
a Java interface, or a Go

00:16:15.570 --> 00:16:16.390
interface, really.

00:16:16.390 --> 00:16:19.010
It's just the interface
with the method names

00:16:19.010 --> 00:16:20.340
and the inputs and the outputs.

00:16:20.340 --> 00:16:23.940
And then you're going to
auto generate the code

00:16:23.940 --> 00:16:25.520
for the client and the servers.

00:16:25.520 --> 00:16:27.520
And then the server is
going to be like, oh, you

00:16:27.520 --> 00:16:30.481
have to actually define whatever
"Hello, World" actually does,

00:16:30.481 --> 00:16:30.980
right?

00:16:30.980 --> 00:16:31.813
VARUN TALWAR: Right.

00:16:31.813 --> 00:16:32.620
MARK MANDEL: Cool.

00:16:32.620 --> 00:16:33.080
VARUN TALWAR: That sounds good.

00:16:33.080 --> 00:16:33.770
MARK MANDEL: That sounds good.

00:16:33.770 --> 00:16:34.310
All right, cool.

00:16:34.310 --> 00:16:35.740
And then so once you've
generated that code,

00:16:35.740 --> 00:16:37.725
it gives you a server that
you can run in that language.

00:16:37.725 --> 00:16:38.870
And then you can start it.

00:16:38.870 --> 00:16:40.390
And then you've got a client
that you can interact with,

00:16:40.390 --> 00:16:40.820
as well.

00:16:40.820 --> 00:16:41.130
VARUN TALWAR: Yeah.

00:16:41.130 --> 00:16:42.750
So you call in
from client stubs,

00:16:42.750 --> 00:16:45.500
and then you test and deploy it.

00:16:45.500 --> 00:16:46.510
MARK MANDEL: Excellent.

00:16:46.510 --> 00:16:48.259
So we touched a little
bit on before-- you

00:16:48.259 --> 00:16:50.650
said 100 times sort of thing.

00:16:50.650 --> 00:16:52.370
I know performance depends.

00:16:52.370 --> 00:16:54.435
But like latency
performance, have

00:16:54.435 --> 00:16:57.290
you seen any sort of
benchmarks, or something

00:16:57.290 --> 00:17:00.590
that you've seen that sort of
gives people at least somewhat

00:17:00.590 --> 00:17:03.369
of a sense of how fast
this thing can really go?

00:17:03.369 --> 00:17:04.160
VARUN TALWAR: Yeah.

00:17:04.160 --> 00:17:08.579
So performance is a tough
one to answer because--

00:17:08.579 --> 00:17:09.579
MARK MANDEL: It depends.

00:17:09.579 --> 00:17:11.662
VARUN TALWAR: Depends is
the right answer, always.

00:17:11.662 --> 00:17:15.540
It's a universally right answer
for performance questions.

00:17:15.540 --> 00:17:22.349
But we have seen,
in all the-- I would

00:17:22.349 --> 00:17:23.960
say three kinds of use cases.

00:17:23.960 --> 00:17:28.430
One is service to services
in microservices world.

00:17:28.430 --> 00:17:31.480
We have seen an
example from CodeOS,

00:17:31.480 --> 00:17:37.140
who is using gRPC extensively
in the distributed k-values

00:17:37.140 --> 00:17:38.190
to [INAUDIBLE].

00:17:38.190 --> 00:17:42.180
Which is also, actually,
one of the underpinnings

00:17:42.180 --> 00:17:44.100
in Kubernetes.

00:17:44.100 --> 00:17:46.850
And they actually
put out a blog post--

00:17:46.850 --> 00:17:51.320
I think it was the end of
last year, December of '15,

00:17:51.320 --> 00:17:54.010
where they compared
it with JSON RPC.

00:17:54.010 --> 00:18:01.070
And, you know, this is
simple put requests for etcd.

00:18:01.070 --> 00:18:03.450
Which, for JSON RPC
was taking anything

00:18:03.450 --> 00:18:06.690
in the order of about
250 to 300 milliseconds,

00:18:06.690 --> 00:18:09.280
was then taking
about 6 milliseconds.

00:18:09.280 --> 00:18:09.781
So you can--

00:18:09.781 --> 00:18:10.488
MARK MANDEL: Wow.

00:18:10.488 --> 00:18:11.030
It's nice.

00:18:11.030 --> 00:18:11.821
VARUN TALWAR: Yeah.

00:18:11.821 --> 00:18:14.330
And the [INAUDIBLE] pushes
out there in the public.

00:18:14.330 --> 00:18:16.206
FRANCESC CAMPOY: That's
like 40 times faster.

00:18:16.206 --> 00:18:16.955
MARK MANDEL: Yeah.

00:18:16.955 --> 00:18:17.970
VARUN TALWAR: Yeah, so--

00:18:17.970 --> 00:18:19.261
FRANCESC CAMPOY: That's decent.

00:18:19.261 --> 00:18:20.170
MARK MANDEL: Yeah.

00:18:20.170 --> 00:18:22.580
VARUN TALWAR: The other
example I can give

00:18:22.580 --> 00:18:26.060
is all-- a lot of
Google Cloud Services

00:18:26.060 --> 00:18:31.300
are exposing a gRPC interface,
as well, in addition to REST.

00:18:31.300 --> 00:18:32.540
Right?

00:18:32.540 --> 00:18:34.980
Some, like BigTable,
are only gRPC.

00:18:34.980 --> 00:18:36.980
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Which
makes sense if you really

00:18:36.980 --> 00:18:38.430
care about the performance.

00:18:38.430 --> 00:18:40.990
VARUN TALWAR: If you're somebody
like Bigtable and PubSearch,

00:18:40.990 --> 00:18:47.210
like high performance throughput
and QPS sort of heavy systems,

00:18:47.210 --> 00:18:49.250
then those are
the services which

00:18:49.250 --> 00:18:52.415
are seeing huge advantages
from having a gRPC interface.

00:18:55.030 --> 00:18:57.470
And the third one
I would say is--

00:18:57.470 --> 00:19:01.560
which a lot of Google
services themselves,

00:19:01.560 --> 00:19:07.250
including our core
mobile apps, are actually

00:19:07.250 --> 00:19:11.930
in the process of
switching to a gRPC,

00:19:11.930 --> 00:19:14.750
which, by the end
of the year or so,

00:19:14.750 --> 00:19:18.440
I think we will have some
real numbers around what that

00:19:18.440 --> 00:19:19.160
has been.

00:19:19.160 --> 00:19:22.060
But we're super excited
with that journey

00:19:22.060 --> 00:19:23.370
and the prospect of that.

00:19:23.370 --> 00:19:25.140
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Nice.

00:19:25.140 --> 00:19:27.730
So I wanted to ask one
question, because I

00:19:27.730 --> 00:19:29.580
keep on getting that
question over and over.

00:19:29.580 --> 00:19:32.197
And if we don't ask it now,
it will be the question--

00:19:32.197 --> 00:19:33.280
MARK MANDEL: It'l show up.

00:19:33.280 --> 00:19:34.480
FRANCESC CAMPOY: The
question of the week anyway.

00:19:34.480 --> 00:19:36.502
MARK MANDEL: It'll show
up, yeah, in the bottom.

00:19:36.502 --> 00:19:37.960
FRANCESC CAMPOY:
Which is-- OK, how

00:19:37.960 --> 00:19:41.320
do you manage multiple
versions of an API?

00:19:41.320 --> 00:19:43.020
That's something
that you have to do.

00:19:43.020 --> 00:19:45.090
And of course one of
the options is like,

00:19:45.090 --> 00:19:47.560
well, never do breaking changes.

00:19:47.560 --> 00:19:48.400
Of course.

00:19:48.400 --> 00:19:50.680
But in the real world,
you will have to do them.

00:19:50.680 --> 00:19:52.690
How do you manage
that with gRPC?

00:19:52.690 --> 00:19:54.090
VARUN TALWAR: Right.

00:19:54.090 --> 00:20:00.680
So this is an area where proto
has actually shined and done

00:20:00.680 --> 00:20:04.260
this well, within
Google, as well as

00:20:04.260 --> 00:20:06.560
people who use
protobuf outside would

00:20:06.560 --> 00:20:09.820
know that it sort of gives
you backward compatibility.

00:20:09.820 --> 00:20:14.390
So for the-- if you've
written protobuf files,

00:20:14.390 --> 00:20:17.860
you've seen that there's a
unique sequence number attached

00:20:17.860 --> 00:20:19.830
with every field.

00:20:19.830 --> 00:20:21.690
And the great
thing with protobuf

00:20:21.690 --> 00:20:25.400
is that you can add new
fields to your message formats

00:20:25.400 --> 00:20:28.090
without ever breaking
backwards compatibility.

00:20:28.090 --> 00:20:32.480
So, you know, old
binaries are services

00:20:32.480 --> 00:20:35.850
that don't know what those
fields are or what they mean.

00:20:35.850 --> 00:20:37.390
Simply ignore it, right?

00:20:37.390 --> 00:20:42.120
So thereby, you can basically
serve multiple versions

00:20:42.120 --> 00:20:45.860
of your services to different
clients at the same time,

00:20:45.860 --> 00:20:51.380
without a huge cost of change,
or without breaking changes

00:20:51.380 --> 00:20:52.289
to existing clients.

00:20:52.289 --> 00:20:53.080
MARK MANDEL: I see.

00:20:53.080 --> 00:20:54.510
VARUN TALWAR: RIght?

00:20:54.510 --> 00:20:58.370
So with proto, that's
a great thing, right?

00:20:58.370 --> 00:21:01.460
And this happens all
the time at Google.

00:21:01.460 --> 00:21:05.330
People update the
protos, push them in.

00:21:05.330 --> 00:21:08.860
And the great thing is
all existing clients

00:21:08.860 --> 00:21:11.082
continue to work.

00:21:11.082 --> 00:21:12.540
Those who don't
understand or don't

00:21:12.540 --> 00:21:15.194
know what to do with the
new fields just ignore them.

00:21:15.194 --> 00:21:16.110
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Nice.

00:21:16.110 --> 00:21:16.670
OK.

00:21:16.670 --> 00:21:18.086
MARK MANDEL: So
you could do stuff

00:21:18.086 --> 00:21:19.810
where like if you
added a field, and then

00:21:19.810 --> 00:21:21.110
somebody sent in a message,
and you're like, oh,

00:21:21.110 --> 00:21:22.950
that's missing, well then I'm
going to take this code down

00:21:22.950 --> 00:21:23.449
this path.

00:21:23.449 --> 00:21:25.730
But if it is there, then
I will do the new thing,

00:21:25.730 --> 00:21:27.030
whatever that happens to be.

00:21:27.030 --> 00:21:29.450
And you can kind of build in
your cases as you need to.

00:21:29.450 --> 00:21:30.283
VARUN TALWAR: Right.

00:21:30.283 --> 00:21:30.890
Right.

00:21:30.890 --> 00:21:32.140
MARK MANDEL: That makes sense.

00:21:32.140 --> 00:21:33.710
That makes a lot of sense.

00:21:33.710 --> 00:21:35.530
OK, cool.

00:21:35.530 --> 00:21:37.180
FRANCESC CAMPOY:
Cool So you also

00:21:37.180 --> 00:21:41.460
mentioned that there
was-- we were actually

00:21:41.460 --> 00:21:44.210
trying to make gRPC something
that is Mobile First.

00:21:44.210 --> 00:21:45.960
And for Mobile First,
there's a couple

00:21:45.960 --> 00:21:48.420
of things which is-- yeah,
performance is important,

00:21:48.420 --> 00:21:51.890
but also data is
expensive and slow.

00:21:51.890 --> 00:21:54.480
So could you talk a
little bit about that?

00:21:54.480 --> 00:21:57.320
Like what's the difference
between gRPC and other things?

00:21:57.320 --> 00:22:04.780
Like maybe just a blob, or
maybe JSON or maybe sheer XML.

00:22:04.780 --> 00:22:07.520
It's got to be smaller
than XML, or JExpect.

00:22:07.520 --> 00:22:09.570
MARK MANDEL: Or JSON
XML, which is a standard.

00:22:09.570 --> 00:22:10.736
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Oh, really?

00:22:10.736 --> 00:22:11.980
MARK MANDEL: Yes.

00:22:11.980 --> 00:22:14.272
VARUN TALWAR: I really don't
want to know what that is.

00:22:14.272 --> 00:22:16.604
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Yeah, so
could you discuss a little bit

00:22:16.604 --> 00:22:17.130
about that?

00:22:17.130 --> 00:22:17.921
VARUN TALWAR: Yeah.

00:22:17.921 --> 00:22:25.680
So from the get go,
we wanted to have

00:22:25.680 --> 00:22:30.230
gRPC be designed in a way
that puts mobile first.

00:22:30.230 --> 00:22:32.950
So there are a few
answers, right?

00:22:32.950 --> 00:22:34.940
Like one, which is
straightforward,

00:22:34.940 --> 00:22:41.350
is basically both JSON and XML
sort of do Base64 encoding.

00:22:41.350 --> 00:22:46.850
So instead of that with
binary over the wire,

00:22:46.850 --> 00:22:51.100
that is where you get huge
efficiencies and advantages

00:22:51.100 --> 00:22:52.120
from.

00:22:52.120 --> 00:22:54.310
What does it mean
for mobile clients?

00:22:54.310 --> 00:22:58.650
It means less data usage.

00:22:58.650 --> 00:23:06.060
It means battery savings, which
are critical in today's times.

00:23:06.060 --> 00:23:11.880
And especially if you're doing
things like real-time events

00:23:11.880 --> 00:23:15.080
on your mobile apps, which
are both client to server

00:23:15.080 --> 00:23:17.340
as well as a server
to client, this

00:23:17.340 --> 00:23:19.960
is where, again, I think
bi-directional streaming

00:23:19.960 --> 00:23:22.900
from gRPC helps a lot.

00:23:22.900 --> 00:23:28.400
So in terms of
actual differences

00:23:28.400 --> 00:23:34.120
with respect to JSON or XML,
or JSON XML, if that exists--

00:23:34.120 --> 00:23:36.120
MARK MANDEL: [INAUDIBLE]

00:23:38.441 --> 00:23:39.940
VARUN TALWAR:
Obviously, it depends,

00:23:39.940 --> 00:23:42.270
in terms of actual
difference we get.

00:23:42.270 --> 00:23:43.850
It depends on the use case.

00:23:43.850 --> 00:23:49.390
But if you go look at our
documentation around protobuf,

00:23:49.390 --> 00:23:55.210
just binary serialization I
think is-- it's 3 to 10 times

00:23:55.210 --> 00:23:58.510
smaller than XML serialization
of structured data,

00:23:58.510 --> 00:24:02.330
and anywhere from 20 to 100
times faster in terms of time

00:24:02.330 --> 00:24:04.160
to process.

00:24:04.160 --> 00:24:07.510
So remember, a
lot of these, it's

00:24:07.510 --> 00:24:09.040
not just about the payload size.

00:24:09.040 --> 00:24:12.360
It's also about
the time to parse,

00:24:12.360 --> 00:24:15.460
which-- and you'll remember your
sort of good old days of SOAPs

00:24:15.460 --> 00:24:16.784
and WSDL and XML parsing.

00:24:16.784 --> 00:24:17.700
MARK MANDEL: Oh, yeah.

00:24:17.700 --> 00:24:18.030
VARUN TALWAR: Right?

00:24:18.030 --> 00:24:20.050
So that is where a
lot of the development

00:24:20.050 --> 00:24:24.560
cost-- not just CPU cycles
cost used to be spent.

00:24:24.560 --> 00:24:30.510
So I think huge efficiencies
to sort of gain from that.

00:24:30.510 --> 00:24:31.261
MARK MANDEL: Nice.

00:24:31.261 --> 00:24:33.760
Seems like it would be a really
good fit for almost anywhere

00:24:33.760 --> 00:24:36.140
that like-- we tend to think
in sort of web applications,

00:24:36.140 --> 00:24:36.639
as well.

00:24:36.639 --> 00:24:38.460
But anywhere that
needs low latency.

00:24:38.460 --> 00:24:39.460
Like I'm thinking IoT.

00:24:39.460 --> 00:24:40.590
We sort of touched on that.

00:24:40.590 --> 00:24:40.960
VARUN TALWAR: Right.

00:24:40.960 --> 00:24:42.000
MARK MANDEL: Obviously
mobile as well.

00:24:42.000 --> 00:24:43.791
Gaming could potentially
be another option.

00:24:43.791 --> 00:24:46.360
So there's a lot of places
that that could definitely fit.

00:24:46.360 --> 00:24:47.151
VARUN TALWAR: Yeah.

00:24:47.151 --> 00:24:50.310
I think for mobile.

00:24:50.310 --> 00:24:53.230
So [INAUDIBLE], I
think we said it,

00:24:53.230 --> 00:24:55.760
which is Objective C for
iOS and Android Java.

00:24:55.760 --> 00:25:01.610
Both are something that
natively are supported for gRPC.

00:25:01.610 --> 00:25:07.170
And we're seeing-- in fact, the
first kinds of use cases we're

00:25:07.170 --> 00:25:10.170
seeing for mobile
apps are all around

00:25:10.170 --> 00:25:13.360
either bidirectional streaming
or chat kind of use cases.

00:25:16.830 --> 00:25:20.710
Or wherever payload
is heavily nested

00:25:20.710 --> 00:25:24.010
in sort of a complicated
data structure

00:25:24.010 --> 00:25:26.470
and nested-- deeply
nested payloads

00:25:26.470 --> 00:25:29.260
is where you'll see
the biggest advantages

00:25:29.260 --> 00:25:31.130
of binary serialization.

00:25:31.130 --> 00:25:33.220
MARK MANDEL: Nice.

00:25:33.220 --> 00:25:33.810
Very nice.

00:25:33.810 --> 00:25:35.410
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Very cool.

00:25:35.410 --> 00:25:37.569
So I actually have a
couple more questions.

00:25:37.569 --> 00:25:38.360
MARK MANDEL: Go on.

00:25:38.360 --> 00:25:40.980
FRANCESC CAMPOY: OK,
so one of the questions

00:25:40.980 --> 00:25:44.940
is-- it's actually a
double-edged question, which

00:25:44.940 --> 00:25:49.020
is, OK, so why could
I do this instead

00:25:49.020 --> 00:25:52.327
of just sending binary blobs,
binary encoding, right?

00:25:52.327 --> 00:25:53.160
VARUN TALWAR: Right.

00:25:53.160 --> 00:25:55.180
FRANCESC CAMPOY: So
why don't I just like

00:25:55.180 --> 00:25:58.190
whatever like in Java--
serialize and just send that?

00:25:58.190 --> 00:25:59.906
VARUN TALWAR: Yeah.

00:25:59.906 --> 00:26:01.880
And, you know, each
of the languages

00:26:01.880 --> 00:26:04.550
have their own such framework.

00:26:04.550 --> 00:26:06.110
Like Java has RMI library.

00:26:06.110 --> 00:26:07.664
MARK MANDEL: Oh, the nightmares.

00:26:07.664 --> 00:26:11.496
[LAUGHTER]

00:26:11.496 --> 00:26:16.690
VARUN TALWAR: So there's a
whole lot of answers to that,

00:26:16.690 --> 00:26:24.040
but primarily being
the ability to-- it's

00:26:24.040 --> 00:26:25.710
not just serializing
on the wire,

00:26:25.710 --> 00:26:29.420
but a standard way
to specify and share

00:26:29.420 --> 00:26:34.070
services, a standard
way to upgrade and run

00:26:34.070 --> 00:26:39.850
backward compatible services,
and a standard way where

00:26:39.850 --> 00:26:41.800
not just how
services communicate,

00:26:41.800 --> 00:26:47.140
but how services do interesting
things like health checking,

00:26:47.140 --> 00:26:50.880
monitoring, tracing,
stats around them.

00:26:50.880 --> 00:26:56.260
This is where one simplified
approach for Google

00:26:56.260 --> 00:27:02.740
has given it a lot of benefits
and has allowed it to really do

00:27:02.740 --> 00:27:06.740
a great amount of efficiency
around reliability, security,

00:27:06.740 --> 00:27:08.350
operations
management, analytics,

00:27:08.350 --> 00:27:10.430
which all become uniform.

00:27:10.430 --> 00:27:13.940
And suddenly,
developers don't have

00:27:13.940 --> 00:27:16.609
to think, well, how will I
talk to that system, right?

00:27:16.609 --> 00:27:18.650
You don't have to think,
oh, which languages I've

00:27:18.650 --> 00:27:19.820
written in?

00:27:19.820 --> 00:27:25.430
I can tell you my personal
story, right out of college

00:27:25.430 --> 00:27:29.470
writing a C backend which
talked to a Java-based front-end

00:27:29.470 --> 00:27:32.345
and trying to hook JNI
libraries and always crashing.

00:27:32.345 --> 00:27:34.327
MARK MANDEL: Ouch.

00:27:34.327 --> 00:27:36.160
VARUN TALWAR: And being
very specific to how

00:27:36.160 --> 00:27:37.600
two languages communicate.

00:27:37.600 --> 00:27:41.630
So I think the-- if you
can take that worry out

00:27:41.630 --> 00:27:44.700
and make sure it
works efficiently,

00:27:44.700 --> 00:27:46.000
that's a huge, huge plus.

00:27:46.000 --> 00:27:47.070
MARK MANDEL: Yeah.

00:27:47.070 --> 00:27:49.170
VARUN TALWAR: And you
don't have to worry about,

00:27:49.170 --> 00:27:51.850
oh, is the Java
out of my library

00:27:51.850 --> 00:27:54.110
supports this kind
of serialization

00:27:54.110 --> 00:27:56.420
and supports this structure,
but the one I'm talking to

00:27:56.420 --> 00:27:57.520
actually does not.

00:27:57.520 --> 00:27:58.330
So how do I--

00:27:58.330 --> 00:27:59.246
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Yeah.

00:27:59.246 --> 00:28:01.375
And I'm assuming that
also versioning--

00:28:01.375 --> 00:28:05.420
like we were talking about
versioning on binary blobs.

00:28:05.420 --> 00:28:06.107
Just forget it.

00:28:06.107 --> 00:28:06.940
VARUN TALWAR: Right.

00:28:06.940 --> 00:28:07.930
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Not even try.

00:28:07.930 --> 00:28:08.350
VARUN TALWAR: Yeah.

00:28:08.350 --> 00:28:09.710
MARK MANDEL: So you've got
like this whole ecosystem

00:28:09.710 --> 00:28:11.620
around things, and then you
can also sit back and be like,

00:28:11.620 --> 00:28:13.300
OK, I'm going to use
the language that's

00:28:13.300 --> 00:28:15.186
the right tool for
this job, and I

00:28:15.186 --> 00:28:17.310
don't have to worry about
any communication at all.

00:28:17.310 --> 00:28:18.143
VARUN TALWAR: Right.

00:28:18.143 --> 00:28:20.925
And any new services
that keep coming up.

00:28:20.925 --> 00:28:21.970
I have a new mobile app.

00:28:21.970 --> 00:28:24.600
I write something
new for IoT devices.

00:28:24.600 --> 00:28:26.400
They just don't have
to worry about how

00:28:26.400 --> 00:28:28.870
I will talk to my
existing and new services.

00:28:28.870 --> 00:28:30.780
MARK MANDEL: Excellent.

00:28:30.780 --> 00:28:31.910
You had two questions.

00:28:31.910 --> 00:28:32.320
FRANCESC CAMPOY: I have?

00:28:32.320 --> 00:28:34.040
Oh yeah, so my
other question was--

00:28:34.040 --> 00:28:34.820
[LAUGHTER]

00:28:34.820 --> 00:28:36.570
I was like why is he
