00:28:44.000 --> 00:28:48.760
is why-- if I'm using
protocol buffers,

00:28:48.760 --> 00:28:52.112
I heard about this other
thing, FlatBuffers.

00:28:52.112 --> 00:28:53.070
What is the difference?

00:28:53.070 --> 00:28:54.740
Why I cannot use FlatBuffers?

00:28:54.740 --> 00:28:57.170
Or can I use
FlatBuffers with gRPC?

00:28:57.170 --> 00:28:58.270
VARUN TALWAR: You can.

00:28:58.270 --> 00:28:59.090
So--

00:28:59.090 --> 00:29:00.590
FRANCESC CAMPOY:
Actually, could you

00:29:00.590 --> 00:29:03.210
explain also what FlatBuffers
is for our listeners?

00:29:03.210 --> 00:29:07.640
VARUN TALWAR: So there are
various sort of variations

00:29:07.640 --> 00:29:10.440
of protocol buffer out there.

00:29:10.440 --> 00:29:17.110
One is, as Frencesc mentioned,
FlatBuffers, which is basically

00:29:17.110 --> 00:29:21.880
trying to have--
at a concert level,

00:29:21.880 --> 00:29:25.170
basically having data structure
represented the same way

00:29:25.170 --> 00:29:27.220
in memory and in disk.

00:29:27.220 --> 00:29:29.060
So you kind of
don't have to take

00:29:29.060 --> 00:29:34.090
on this conversion serialization
and deserialization cost.

00:29:34.090 --> 00:29:37.317
And, you know, something
there is picking up.

00:29:37.317 --> 00:29:39.650
There is also another version
of protocol buffers called

00:29:39.650 --> 00:29:46.590
nano protocol buffers, which is
more for IoT kind of scenarios,

00:29:46.590 --> 00:29:51.220
making the payload even
smaller and even lighter.

00:29:51.220 --> 00:29:55.680
So one great thing about
gRPC in terms of its design--

00:29:55.680 --> 00:29:58.530
and, you know, kudos
to all the engineers

00:29:58.530 --> 00:30:01.522
who started with
the design of it--

00:30:01.522 --> 00:30:05.710
is it is designed to
be IDL agnostic, right?

00:30:05.710 --> 00:30:09.940
So, in theory, you can swap
in an IDL of your choice.

00:30:09.940 --> 00:30:14.380
Instead of proto, you can
use FlatBuffers, or NanoPB,

00:30:14.380 --> 00:30:18.180
or Thrift, or other
IDLs, for that matter.

00:30:18.180 --> 00:30:22.250
And it is-- you can swap it out.

00:30:22.250 --> 00:30:23.560
There is some work on that.

00:30:23.560 --> 00:30:27.650
But you could, in concept,
use it across IDLs.

00:30:27.650 --> 00:30:30.110
And while I'm giving
kudos on design,

00:30:30.110 --> 00:30:32.120
the other thing is,
it's not just IDL

00:30:32.120 --> 00:30:35.610
agnostic, but transport
protocol agnostic.

00:30:35.610 --> 00:30:41.020
So you could, in theory,
swap in HTTP/2 with QUIC

00:30:41.020 --> 00:30:45.090
and get-- you know, do
smart things like that.

00:30:45.090 --> 00:30:47.725
Which, for those of you
who don't know QUIC,

00:30:47.725 --> 00:30:51.290
is an implementation
on top of UDP.

00:30:51.290 --> 00:31:00.490
And some high media payload
use cases like YouTube

00:31:00.490 --> 00:31:03.284
are really trying to
take advantage of those.

00:31:03.284 --> 00:31:04.200
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Yeah.

00:31:04.200 --> 00:31:06.620
We had an episode
with Ilya Grigorik

00:31:06.620 --> 00:31:10.067
and he discussed HTTP/2
and QUIC a little bit.

00:31:10.067 --> 00:31:11.400
VARUN TALWAR: That was episode--

00:31:11.400 --> 00:31:11.770
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Yes.

00:31:11.770 --> 00:31:13.350
VARUN TALWAR: I don't remember.

00:31:13.350 --> 00:31:13.870
MARK MANDEL: Definitely, yes.

00:31:13.870 --> 00:31:14.930
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Yeah.

00:31:14.930 --> 00:31:17.070
For any listener that
is listening to this,

00:31:17.070 --> 00:31:18.625
just find that Ilya
Grigorik episode

00:31:18.625 --> 00:31:21.310
to-- if you want to learn
more about QUIC and HTTP/2.

00:31:21.310 --> 00:31:22.560
MARK MANDEL: Yeah, definitely.

00:31:22.560 --> 00:31:23.830
Definitely.

00:31:23.830 --> 00:31:24.460
Awesome.

