00:38:20.550 --> 00:38:22.910
FRANCESC CAMPOY: So yeah,
David is a developer advocate

00:38:22.910 --> 00:38:24.090
for the Firebase team.

00:38:24.090 --> 00:38:26.560
And I think that you
had a pretty good answer

00:38:26.560 --> 00:38:27.290
to this question.

00:38:27.290 --> 00:38:28.667
Should I create references?

00:38:28.667 --> 00:38:29.750
Should I pass them around?

00:38:29.750 --> 00:38:30.614
What do I do?

00:38:30.614 --> 00:38:32.030
DAVID EAST: Well,
a lot of people,

00:38:32.030 --> 00:38:33.488
when they're first
new to Firebase,

00:38:33.488 --> 00:38:37.120
they think, all right, when
I create this Firebase class,

00:38:37.120 --> 00:38:39.345
initialize it, it's
kind of like a database.

00:38:39.345 --> 00:38:41.704
So it's really
resource intensive.

00:38:41.704 --> 00:38:43.120
So I want to keep
it around, and I

00:38:43.120 --> 00:38:45.340
want to make sure
that I'm not just

00:38:45.340 --> 00:38:48.760
creating these big objects
that consume all this memory

00:38:48.760 --> 00:38:51.060
or suck up all this data.

00:38:51.060 --> 00:38:54.130
And that's not really the case
with the Firebase reference.

00:38:54.130 --> 00:38:57.690
Because when your app first
connects to your Firebase

00:38:57.690 --> 00:39:00.480
database, that creates
a persistent web socket

00:39:00.480 --> 00:39:03.760
from your app to the
Firebase servers.

00:39:03.760 --> 00:39:07.100
And when you create
another reference,

00:39:07.100 --> 00:39:09.550
it doesn't just go off and
create another WebSocket.

00:39:09.550 --> 00:39:12.900
The Firebase SDK just shares
on that, the original socket

00:39:12.900 --> 00:39:15.760
built. So there's only ever one
socket connected to the server.

00:39:15.760 --> 00:39:18.135
So you don't have to worry
about like, oh, new reference,

00:39:18.135 --> 00:39:19.410
new socket, new problems.

00:39:19.410 --> 00:39:21.500
Like, no, it all
shares through the one.

00:39:21.500 --> 00:39:25.710
So the Firebase SDK
handles that for you.

00:39:25.710 --> 00:39:28.220
As far as creating
them, you can create

00:39:28.220 --> 00:39:29.860
really as many as you want.

00:39:29.860 --> 00:39:33.389
Like that's not going to be
the memory intensive part.

00:39:33.389 --> 00:39:34.930
The memory intensive
part is actually

00:39:34.930 --> 00:39:38.990
observing the real time
listeners for the data.

00:39:38.990 --> 00:39:42.190
And that's really only as memory
intensive as how much data

00:39:42.190 --> 00:39:43.390
you're trying to sync back.

00:39:43.390 --> 00:39:45.280
Because that's-- bandwidth
is just bandwidth,

00:39:45.280 --> 00:39:47.780
and slow internet connections
are slow internet connections.

00:39:47.780 --> 00:39:51.220
So there's not too much
we can do about that.

00:39:51.220 --> 00:39:54.640
But otherwise, you're totally
fine with just creating

00:39:54.640 --> 00:39:56.980
a new reference inside
another view control,

00:39:56.980 --> 00:39:59.740
or something like that,
and just synchronizing

00:39:59.740 --> 00:40:01.450
that data to your views.

00:40:01.450 --> 00:40:03.770
You shouldn't really be
using Firebase inside

00:40:03.770 --> 00:40:05.490
of View, because
that messes up, you

00:40:05.490 --> 00:40:08.480
know, the sacred NVC of iOS.

00:40:08.480 --> 00:40:10.760
But still, you
don't have to worry

00:40:10.760 --> 00:40:12.194
about just keeping one around.

00:40:12.194 --> 00:40:13.860
MARK MANDEL: Yeah,
so it's not like when

00:40:13.860 --> 00:40:15.240
you think about
server applications,

00:40:15.240 --> 00:40:17.125
where you have a database
connection with pooling,

00:40:17.125 --> 00:40:19.540
and it's very resource intensive
and has all these connections.

00:40:19.540 --> 00:40:21.020
It's much more
lightweight than that.

00:40:21.020 --> 00:40:22.103
DAVID EAST: Yeah, exactly.

00:40:22.103 --> 00:40:25.470
FRANCESC CAMPOY: So the
answer for this participant

00:40:25.470 --> 00:40:29.536
could be, when you sign in,
you're going to get a UID.

00:40:29.536 --> 00:40:30.660
And you create a reference.

00:40:30.660 --> 00:40:32.100
Rather than creating
the reference

00:40:32.100 --> 00:40:33.510
and then just passing
it around, just

00:40:33.510 --> 00:40:35.370
pass the UID, which is
a simple [INAUDIBLE].

00:40:35.370 --> 00:40:37.650
DAVID EAST: Actually, it
gets even easier than that.

00:40:37.650 --> 00:40:39.920
You don't have actually
have to pass the UID around.

00:40:39.920 --> 00:40:44.110
Because once you have an
authenticated user in Firebase,

00:40:44.110 --> 00:40:46.965
you can actually-- for
iOS, you can say whatever.

00:40:46.965 --> 00:40:48.090
You create a new reference.

00:40:48.090 --> 00:40:49.450
The variable name is "ref."

00:40:49.450 --> 00:40:51.640
So you say ref.authdata.

00:40:51.640 --> 00:40:54.400
And as long as that user-- you
have a currently authenticated

00:40:54.400 --> 00:40:57.404
user, that off data
property will be populated

00:40:57.404 --> 00:40:58.320
with the current user.

00:40:58.320 --> 00:41:00.480
So you can say ref.authdata.uid.

00:41:00.480 --> 00:41:02.330
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Oh.

00:41:02.330 --> 00:41:04.400
DAVID EAST: So I
actually never have

00:41:04.400 --> 00:41:06.460
to carry around any of
the user information,

00:41:06.460 --> 00:41:08.937
because it's always available
on any reference you create.

00:41:08.937 --> 00:41:09.770
FRANCESC CAMPOY: OK.

00:41:09.770 --> 00:41:11.380
That's pretty awesome indeed.

00:41:11.380 --> 00:41:12.410
DAVID EAST: Yeah.

00:41:12.410 --> 00:41:13.080
FRANCESC CAMPOY:
There was actually

00:41:13.080 --> 00:41:15.170
a second part of the
question, which was like, OK,

00:41:15.170 --> 00:41:17.470
